WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.360
Charles Blagden went into a room heated to about 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

2
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.440
A thermometer under his tongue read 98 degrees, about 1 degree above his usual.

3
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.080
An egg in hot air heats until it matches the air.

4
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.560
A living person did not.

5
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:28.883
Biologists call this homeostasis, the holding of a body variable within a range

6
00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:30.623
compatible with staying alive.

7
00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:34.023
Every loop that does it has five parts.

8
00:00:34.663 --> 00:00:39.523
The regulated variable is the quantity held, and the set point is its reference

9
00:00:39.523 --> 00:00:45.063
value. The sensor measures the variable, and the integrator compares the sensed

10
00:00:45.063 --> 00:00:47.503
value with the set point and sends a command.

11
00:00:47.503 --> 00:00:52.843
The effector is a muscle, gland or other organ that changes the variable.

12
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:58.013
In an adult human, the variable is core temperature.

13
00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:03.173
Temperature-sensitive nerve cells in the skin and brain are the sensors.

14
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:07.853
The hypothalamus, a region of the brain, is the integrator.

15
00:01:08.673 --> 00:01:13.733
When temperature rises, the effectors are sweat glands, and evaporation of sweat

16
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:19.553
removes heat. When it falls, the effectors include skeletal muscles, which shiver

17
00:01:19.553 --> 00:01:21.853
and release heat as they use ATP.

18
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.967
If temperature rises, sweating brings it down.

19
00:01:26.687 --> 00:01:29.027
If it falls, shivering brings it up.

20
00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:34.867
Both are negative feedback, because each response pushes the variable back toward

21
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:38.307
the set point. The set point itself can move.

22
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:44.387
In a fever it rises, so a person at normal temperature feels cold and shivers.

23
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:51.097
A regulator holds a variable fairly steady across outside conditions, and a

24
00:01:51.097 --> 00:01:53.297
conformer lets it follow the environment.

25
00:01:54.217 --> 00:01:56.837
An adult human regulates core temperature.

26
00:01:57.457 --> 00:02:02.677
A typical teleost fish in a lake is an ectotherm whose body temperature largely

27
00:02:02.677 --> 00:02:03.737
follows the water.

28
00:02:04.337 --> 00:02:07.157
That is a different solution, not a lesser one.

29
00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:14.537
For blood salt, the shore crab conforms in full-strength seawater and regulates in diluted water.

31
00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:17.327
Regulation is not free.

32
00:02:18.247 --> 00:02:23.867
In single-housed male mice of one laboratory strain, energy expenditure was about

33
00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:28.127
30% higher at 22 degrees Celsius than at 30.

34
00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:35.987
A mouse is small and loses heat fast, so at 22 degrees it spends more to stay warm.

35
00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:40.527
Name the variable first, then the loop that holds it.
